 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94867" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406849} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406886}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/