{bmatrix}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454368} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd8a2c9-e002-5140-8dcb-a07eb82acf68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453238} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c0ea91-328a-544b-b2c0-d0c461ef46cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6c0ea91-328a-544b-b2c0-d0c461ef46cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453239} :answer-field/id #uuid "9796340d-a680-530e-86de-58d047300c0a" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-107533" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}=a\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-b)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-c)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+d\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$”; “$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406849} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406888}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bbdee2f-94bd-572f-91ae-a989e989cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454060} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6016de-8322-5611-abac-11c53498b5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bbdee2f-94bd-572f-91ae-a989e989cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454060} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b86c8c7-ce4a-5843-b124-fd92dc984de2" :answer-field/key "${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5061063-49ba-56ef-8537-f424f6dc8a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454062} {:answer/id #uuid "710153ca-da8e-5423-9cbc-1f6713a46398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "710153ca-da8e-5423-9cbc-1f6713a46398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454063} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7647c92-3c6a-55d5-b2a1-537c5b726446" :answer-field/key "${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0743a148-5538-58b8-be74-ca107d1c906d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454064} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c81ac2-78b7-59ca-8a48-01ff07cdd26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0743a148-5538-58b8-be74-ca107d1c906d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccfdb226-5087-511e-8a84-93a1b34f530a" :answer-field/key "${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80631" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ are bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$” is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement “${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement “${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$” is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406849} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406884} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485759} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486522}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050360 :topic/id #uuid "4264930a-5f20-54f0-ad2a-c3ad45c03658" :topic/math-academy-id 250 :topic/title "Conditional Statements"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a11b4773-38d5-5c0c-9e9c-3e0b4ecae818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453132} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab874b5-03d7-53c3-b6db-a652d9c156a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a11b4773-38d5-5c0c-9e9c-3e0b4ecae818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453132} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f8e36a-498b-5385-816f-6d262d6c4893" :answer-field/key "**If** $12$ is divisible by $3$, **then** $9$ is divisible by $4.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454316} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96553132-07aa-5dd8-a9d9-80d5ba1e0f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453134} {:answer/id #uuid "f12b50d9-97e8-547c-ae5c-e9776f0678cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f12b50d9-97e8-547c-ae5c-e9776f0678cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453135} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae81137-1867-50f5-a1a9-e15ecfd57220" :answer-field/key "**If** $5$ is divisible by $2$, **then** $10$ is divisible by $5.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923f9c82-2491-5942-a700-0059eb11d931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453136} {:answer/id #uuid "bca078d7-e26d-5c8c-ac29-1d2b30f25905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca078d7-e26d-5c8c-ac29-1d2b30f25905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453137} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ffb718-a2da-578a-a502-45fd1b77e827" :answer-field/key "**If** $9$ is divisible by $5$, **then** $6$ is divisible by $3.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55e540af-11e2-5ec3-9b47-ab0da3ac740e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103558" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement “**If** $12$ is divisible by $3$, **then** $9$ is divisible by $4.$” is false. The antecedent \"$12$ is divisible by $3$\" is true, and the consequent \"$9$ is divisible by $4$\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement “**If** $5$ is divisible by $2$, **then** $10$ is divisible by $5.$” is true. The antecedent \"$5$ is divisible by $2$\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement “**If** $9$ is divisible by $5$, **then** $6$ is divisible by $3.$” is true. Again, the antecedent \"$9$ is divisible by $5$\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “**If** $5$ is divisible by $2$, **then** $10$ is divisible by $5.$”; “**If** $9$ is divisible by $5$, **then** $6$ is divisible by $3.$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050360 :topic/id #uuid "4264930a-5f20-54f0-ad2a-c3ad45c03658" :topic/math-academy-id 250 :topic/title "Conditional Statements"} :activity/title "Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406924} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406952}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050360 :topic/id #uuid "4264930a-5f20-54f0-ad2a-c3ad45c03658" :topic/math-academy-id 250 :topic/title "Conditional Statements"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20959140-93b8-5a47-a197-da32ff4810aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454066} {:answer/id #uuid "9379cea3-738f-5b45-9ffb-a0d389c84fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9379cea3-738f-5b45-9ffb-a0d389c84fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454067} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc3054a-806e-55e3-a67b-9d3aafb6ab0e" :answer-field/key "**If** $7$ is an even number, **then** $6$ is an odd number." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862f1c15-7d4f-5155-be39-3424fe47a6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454068} {:answer/id #uuid "e30f18bc-42c2-57a7-8e53-faed108c78b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862f1c15-7d4f-5155-be39-3424fe47a6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454068} :answer-field/id #uuid "6338d248-c558-59d6-b0d2-c36e277fc0ef" :answer-field/key "**If** $6$ is an even number, **then** $7$ is an even number." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ff828bb-30f0-59e3-937e-d221fd6fafb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454070} {:answer/id #uuid "5d13fad8-f3e6-549c-954e-a199b234815c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d13fad8-f3e6-549c-954e-a199b234815c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454071} :answer-field/id #uuid "693882b2-096a-516e-95d6-0393826e4576" :answer-field/key "**If** $6$ is an even number, **then** $7$ is an odd number." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "806aafe6-aa9d-53f3-a7d9-19809d388a29" :question/math-academy-id "q-82092" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement “**If** $7$ is an even number, **then** $6$ is an odd number” is true. The antecedent \"$7$ is an even number\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement “**If** $6$ is an even number, **then** $7$ is an even number” is false. The antecedent \"$6$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$7$ is an even number\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement “**If** $6$ is an even number, **then** $7$ is an odd number” is true. The antecedent \"$6$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$7$ is an odd number\" is true. So the promise is kept, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “**If** $7$ is an even number, **then** $6$ is an odd number”; “**If** $6$ is an even number, **then** $7$ is an odd number” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050360 :topic/id #uuid "4264930a-5f20-54f0-ad2a-c3ad45c03658" :topic/math-academy-id 250 :topic/title "Conditional Statements"} :activity/title "Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406924} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406953}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979 :topic/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b532929-031f-53ba-845b-1b69f35ae5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473278} {:answer/id #uuid "da5ae196-6ddc-5f90-8faf-ce02251fa644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da5ae196-6ddc-5f90-8faf-ce02251fa644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473279} :answer-field/id #uuid "8261d6df-4d7c-5438-ab24-46a9a778e5ea" :answer-field/key "It is a type I region" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2626a4-af3b-558c-898b-e325dcd7c8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473280} {:answer/id #uuid "74014f19-619f-54ff-9fc7-8f16f269680a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74014f19-619f-54ff-9fc7-8f16f269680a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473281} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f34c2c1-88e7-5e59-bf72-b638136d3b12" :answer-field/key "It is a type II region" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474035} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d131820-9e7d-5679-bbf5-8ff122bec3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473282} {:answer/id #uuid "38a132f3-eea1-5c88-b25c-151a9517cd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d131820-9e7d-5679-bbf5-8ff122bec3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473282} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f148b1b-a08c-5f92-88d7-fab284af6a47" :answer-field/key "It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d779a1b-dcae-59f0-822c-7860f9483ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72160" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ in the first quadrant?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & ⟹\\,x={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/89/899357795c6d5b81d3cb619d71493ad787645697fe1f2fcecda78c1b875f388e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements “It is a type I region” and “It is a type II region” are true." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979 :topic/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space"} :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406975} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406987} {:activity/_steps {:activity/title "Quiz 8" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527305} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528031}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20f3748-cb10-509d-a2bd-0a0fd23b7eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472844} {:answer/id #uuid "f0900ef4-8b2d-5a5a-98b1-8edf44a8d0fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20f3748-cb10-509d-a2bd-0a0fd23b7eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472844} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d3ca7b-8368-55a5-9437-6bc2f8ee9b8a" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $k$ immediately after $∀$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473817} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a3963e9-5bae-5d39-890a-f4177c4aa4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472846} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3806ef-2d1e-54e7-a983-2f853e1f75da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe3806ef-2d1e-54e7-a983-2f853e1f75da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472847} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a96ae20-3158-56a5-8c42-beb8f31d27c2" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $n$ in $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473818} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21044ff-3035-50a3-b828-93a4ae2dde70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472848} {:answer/id #uuid "25db3510-d2ea-59b7-a47b-63dc65eb24c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b21044ff-3035-50a3-b828-93a4ae2dde70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472848} :answer-field/id #uuid "e06faf13-f34b-5712-a464-83a6b07e81f1" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $k$ on the right side of $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "085a10d3-7324-5921-95c5-eaa8f395984a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183620" :question/problem "$$\n∀{k}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $k$ immediately after $∀$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,n+{k}^{2}\\ge k\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $n$ in $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- The occurrence of $k$ on the right side of $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀k$ that uses $k:$\n\n$$\n∀k\\in (0,1),\\,\\overbrace{n+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∀k}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀\\,{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $n$ in $n+{k}^{2}\\ge k$” only" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"} :activity/title "Nested Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407113} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407136}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6927f03c-aa7a-56ac-8cd0-c036a11aadc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49953372-6786-55fd-b957-4551684db7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453670} {:answer/id #uuid "eb694d2a-846a-5113-a363-79bb2236c246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb694d2a-846a-5113-a363-79bb2236c246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453671} :answer-field/id #uuid "75840e40-2cca-58f4-a515-e9cfdb46f959" :answer-field/key "$∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,2p=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454585} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57c979c3-fafa-584e-abe1-50aaafd5442e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453672} {:answer/id #uuid "374d893b-950f-54cb-a1f5-2cd335ae552b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57c979c3-fafa-584e-abe1-50aaafd5442e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453672} :answer-field/id #uuid "726bad40-3e61-5e83-b318-93789e8c9f1e" :answer-field/key "The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\\,)$ is $Q.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9be598-49fa-52b8-a352-44784bf90317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453674} {:answer/id #uuid "e6cb932f-f971-54a3-ad09-1463e1bb7456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6cb932f-f971-54a3-ad09-1463e1bb7456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453675} :answer-field/id #uuid "9963ef57-4cea-572c-8d46-02df8158a551" :answer-field/key "$∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a806540-0634-5e11-9ba2-ff988650a653" :question/math-academy-id "q-183877" :question/problem "Let $p\\in Q$ and $q\\in Q.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\n$$\n\nfrom option $∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1.$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exist rationals $q$ and $p,$ such that $p\\cdot q=-1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,2p=-1.$ is true, $2p=-1$ for some rational number $p$ (e.g., for $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$).\n\n- Statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\\,)$ is $Q.$” is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(q):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(q)$ is true for some $q\\in Q$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot 0=-1\n$$\n\nis false. However, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nis true, since we can take $p=-1.$\n\n- Statement $∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1.$ is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃q\\in Q\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∃p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1}_{P(q)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(q)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to the discussion in statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\\,)$ is $Q.$”, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,2p=-1.$; $∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1.$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"} :activity/title "Nested Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407113} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407137}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "579250e5-9a15-54a1-9aef-61843d7e73bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472850} {:answer/id #uuid "bac29f65-92c2-5c70-9ac1-938b9b7c9f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "579250e5-9a15-54a1-9aef-61843d7e73bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472850} :answer-field/id #uuid "da4eca5f-8cd7-542e-9644-037ddfda06a8" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∀$ in the left-hand statement" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3dc152-a2ec-569a-8cdb-75853d4eab7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472852} {:answer/id #uuid "46ef506f-4104-5a21-ad5f-afc0dd168aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46ef506f-4104-5a21-ad5f-afc0dd168aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472853} :answer-field/id #uuid "905f7199-6958-58e0-ad1d-a6857970bc53" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $x$ in $x=\\operatorname{sin} y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f281ae76-653f-5d18-a0eb-0fe9727b9b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472854} {:answer/id #uuid "6040f888-8437-5a2a-ad58-c9c0b0b28a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f281ae76-653f-5d18-a0eb-0fe9727b9b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472854} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b222a4-df9e-5d6c-8b68-583b11d65aa7" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $y$ in $\\operatorname{sin} y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39d9d2c3-0d6e-5d14-b069-fbe9f014d46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-183674" :question/problem "$$\n(\\,∀{x}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∀$ in the left-hand statement is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,)\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $x=\\operatorname{sin} y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- The occurrence of $y$ in $\\operatorname{sin} y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃y$ that uses $y:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃y})\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $x$ in $x=\\operatorname{sin} y$” only" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"} :activity/title "Nested Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407113} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407135}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200 :topic/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e37cdb6-c2f8-5f2d-b68e-1ab7dae2c068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472502} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ee36b-6930-5033-bbff-c6e5831b6de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e37cdb6-c2f8-5f2d-b68e-1ab7dae2c068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472502} :answer-field/id #uuid "48015e6d-6aef-53c6-aa13-f63167aef2d9" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c132e320-a78b-5003-a32b-1274e70dff6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472504} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9107f0-649f-5e5e-8c94-d1089de849cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9107f0-649f-5e5e-8c94-d1089de849cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472505} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff283957-040e-592b-8540-faef6177d2d1" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c7e552-c7ec-5bd5-9721-98ed03dbe1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472506} {:answer/id #uuid "bce36cf7-e320-5e6d-97a5-94061b6e55fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49c7e552-c7ec-5bd5-9721-98ed03dbe1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472506} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2b47b3-71ab-5d28-ac5e-96cdddfab7f6" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Null}(A)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498" :question/math-academy-id "q-109777" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A)$ for every matrix $A?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $A$, that is, the rows of ${A}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${A}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${A}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ only.\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200 :topic/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407212} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4106fe70-b9df-5621-ba64-66703855aaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Conjunction Absorbs Disjunction" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050128 :topic/id #uuid "2edfad38-c4ef-5df9-8b35-5ea30d354656" :topic/math-academy-id 2823 :topic/title "The Absorption Laws"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "679af59b-9a9b-5445-a2ed-971d3b27b789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473428} {:answer/id #uuid "de09f7f6-1950-5bcd-bd5c-77f9db8d881f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679af59b-9a9b-5445-a2ed-971d3b27b789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473428} :answer-field/id #uuid "4227a58e-2c13-5157-9e01-c278430e3976" :answer-field/key "$(T∨R)∧T$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd41ee6-ad6b-5890-a457-f6ca7b6dce4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473430} {:answer/id #uuid "02c1ee5c-a4fa-5962-a619-7f070b4cd2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02c1ee5c-a4fa-5962-a619-7f070b4cd2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473431} :answer-field/id #uuid "afee90de-95e4-5385-bed8-47f54fc880ed" :answer-field/key "$R∧(T∨R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6890afd6-d111-51ac-ae72-3d7d329c5b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473432} {:answer/id #uuid "8a46eb9d-07c1-5ef1-b9f1-b6989168e206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a46eb9d-07c1-5ef1-b9f1-b6989168e206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473433} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cced8f6-da3e-5b5c-9853-44c78ca4e503" :answer-field/key "$(R∨T)∧R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "669b0027-14f5-5068-b932-b9686df06625" :question/math-academy-id "q-81717" :question/problem "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nR∧(R∨T)≡R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $(T∨R)∧T$ is *not* logically equivalent to $R.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(T∨R)∧T & ≡T∧(T∨R) \\\\  & ≡T≢R.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $R∧(T∨R)$ is logically equivalent to $R.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R∧(T∨R) & ≡R∧(R∨T) \\\\  & ≡R.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $(R∨T)∧R$ is logically equivalent to $R.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(R∨T)∧R & ≡R∧(R∨T) \\\\  & ≡R.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R∧(T∨R)$; $(R∨T)∧R$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050128 :topic/id #uuid "2edfad38-c4ef-5df9-8b35-5ea30d354656" :topic/math-academy-id 2823 :topic/title "The Absorption Laws"} :activity/title "The Absorption Laws" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407253} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407274}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d87facea-76c9-53bf-b25e-e7acd8a0848f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472616} {:answer/id #uuid "34788671-40c0-55de-9282-705047e61c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34788671-40c0-55de-9282-705047e61c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472617} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cca8d7-a733-550e-ab33-1ebcc8cc6ba3" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb2a430-1b20-5cd8-889c-8397d5f66f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472618} {:answer/id #uuid "facde33f-6a68-5c26-a7d0-f157550ab480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "facde33f-6a68-5c26-a7d0-f157550ab480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472619} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b1f945-9ba0-5e99-9ab4-a0c22108dd3f" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f4df998-df9d-5be1-85e2-6c7fe20e4d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472620} {:answer/id #uuid "94aa333b-da6a-5843-999a-18b8e9d9555f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4df998-df9d-5be1-85e2-6c7fe20e4d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472620} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b668eb0-a01c-5c4c-9775-6cc00549cbbd" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{R}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04b43074-9710-54c1-a656-7d7797008d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-129786" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d66cb97b9e04367ce2250e5156eaa9daaf42fadb395220e0464bca687e91908.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$”; “${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407276} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407299}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d7503-b6f8-527c-a973-ac2bfc867df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472622} {:answer/id #uuid "77d96fda-e870-55c8-b23f-4d1d5eb0e5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d96fda-e870-55c8-b23f-4d1d5eb0e5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472623} :answer-field/id #uuid "256d9380-5a22-5d58-bc62-7da50bb3ece8" :answer-field/key "${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f3b95a-f8ca-5464-96e2-8800e62a2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472624} {:answer/id #uuid "50306918-6a2a-5e3c-a5ab-150d520b00cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f3b95a-f8ca-5464-96e2-8800e62a2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472624} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d53d61-4f6d-56eb-96a8-a48708fcd15a" :answer-field/key "${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642ff713-a98e-574b-ab8d-f4ec58c4a354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472626} {:answer/id #uuid "f07b0134-b33f-5f41-9115-502b58e12756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f07b0134-b33f-5f41-9115-502b58e12756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472627} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f28af97-dc10-5715-ba3e-f26a28eab3f5" :answer-field/key "${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bbe5a871-1924-5655-ad62-5cf32208bd34" :question/math-academy-id "q-129908" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=2-|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/1a/1a5369b58b14444c5ea0ce4bb36954d1d0b7964dcef4e95f9cc527694b3a9e4f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$ and ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$; ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407276} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407302}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d785f01e-c01b-508b-a7e4-0b68e3819afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473512} {:answer/id #uuid "72475eb4-0b35-5d4b-9ea7-4c75ae1d9bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72475eb4-0b35-5d4b-9ea7-4c75ae1d9bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473513} :answer-field/id #uuid "7063df18-38c8-5001-85dc-c37c2a884180" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474151} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45915786-bde2-58ed-b09f-01d02dd4ab1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473514} {:answer/id #uuid "db2767a5-0290-566a-8860-446c19d11819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45915786-bde2-58ed-b09f-01d02dd4ab1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473514} :answer-field/id #uuid "9428d01a-a858-5759-ad6e-dec6825a2e03" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20decd90-c71f-5b9c-af2d-3a173c83d963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473516} {:answer/id #uuid "025119c3-228a-5031-89d8-d102fe48b0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20decd90-c71f-5b9c-af2d-3a173c83d963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473516} :answer-field/id #uuid "31799862-7294-50e6-9e5a-cbd879317060" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed965e5b-f0df-5c0f-9c7c-0cb7a433c067" :question/math-academy-id "q-86866" :question/problem "![](images/51/51706be38cc1c72079fe702c899b575ea53ba4d85e7990a6aa81f20dc8da4de3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407276} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407301}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7adad79-7530-548f-8ef5-d7d669e56a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473314} {:answer/id #uuid "3e638fd2-f5b6-56e4-a961-6af8957d8b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e638fd2-f5b6-56e4-a961-6af8957d8b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473315} :answer-field/id #uuid "a473b682-7626-5413-963d-7ed5605a64f2" :answer-field/key "![Diagram I](images/94/9484c41b7b3141c697d25e72b106f018cb78f2bc68f2d671555c8d347f37f726.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f30a22a7-4bfd-5473-b723-c45d77507594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473316} {:answer/id #uuid "09191b98-cda3-5c87-82cd-aa5bca0c44ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f30a22a7-4bfd-5473-b723-c45d77507594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473316} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a18be48-97e8-5987-8dfc-ecda00586f8b" :answer-field/key "![Diagram II](images/e7/e7d5fdbccc6e9fc256924810e9fb7867fb30a52edad837a7dbb55c3fee857901.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66355ab9-c052-5d61-9dd1-e8de20763389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473318} {:answer/id #uuid "f502b7b1-cfd1-55bc-bf4e-4a5fb1dfba9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66355ab9-c052-5d61-9dd1-e8de20763389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473318} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a32fca9-dae5-5c00-9560-d1306e445f41" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/cf/cf2db417b6f90899d1264ecb763f90c3bb654e37be4abc4221e912b2a4dc09ee.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82f2815d-a760-5a7e-b3a4-18ea182996e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74214" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram I only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86eb0777-d080-53e8-b004-79a014639bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473088} {:answer/id #uuid "7872a4e4-6631-5d82-b92a-e36225c1be50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86eb0777-d080-53e8-b004-79a014639bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473088} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7455588-72d5-550c-9183-e46e0782f4aa" :answer-field/key "![Diagram I](images/53/5303e5de04148fbd414db26930d50d5c0cd4ef19af5a46afaf4b312966cdfded.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79c0d29f-bfc2-506c-909d-d2ee04efe33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473090} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1c9619-2805-5f10-9fdb-01a2befb4460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c0d29f-bfc2-506c-909d-d2ee04efe33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473090} :answer-field/id #uuid "11293092-de1e-51d5-b24b-3326cc42091a" :answer-field/key "![Diagram II](images/cc/ccdca51aef8ee61c23d3f3a81b1e4673598ffdde88cb4f58b4b35bb76886f524.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473940} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee9299db-52e0-589b-b002-e37948bbdadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473092} {:answer/id #uuid "474e300d-b0ad-520c-b70a-93c1e5e2e177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9299db-52e0-589b-b002-e37948bbdadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473092} :answer-field/id #uuid "19219c0d-23df-589d-82c4-a37e066f5d0f" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/e4/e495be81f41917740b46b1bfd43fd5cde69041981463c3dbbd96e55d206a8806.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "befb255b-f1bd-5c00-bc58-981c00e76ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35574" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case we also have that the vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048604 :topic/id #uuid "ade9d1d5-3224-5403-9c8b-f3e5be0db3c5" :topic/math-academy-id 3080 :topic/title "Integer Divisibility"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "737ddb9c-20f6-56c9-b552-48af2b027a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453812} {:answer/id #uuid "8393d104-6db5-5931-886e-bb8328757c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "737ddb9c-20f6-56c9-b552-48af2b027a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453812} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff2d277b-d9cd-5eb1-83d5-2d362b611c63" :answer-field/key "$2∣(60x-45y)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e22bff92-60f3-51f2-9155-f377b93c2d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453814} {:answer/id #uuid "1516d313-d02b-5a99-81f2-fefb1e7579be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22bff92-60f3-51f2-9155-f377b93c2d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453814} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b9332c5-f40f-5b87-93f3-1ad8fb15835e" :answer-field/key "$9∣(60x-45y)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454657} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f457f45-74c8-5f73-94f8-6c124c4c86ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453816} {:answer/id #uuid "2173b88d-3a23-5bda-b24f-5e268299e69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2173b88d-3a23-5bda-b24f-5e268299e69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453817} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b44fa0c-9299-53a1-a23a-0b42b875f862" :answer-field/key "$5∣(60x-45y)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57bcbc75-d195-518f-bc0e-7dcf10346726" :question/math-academy-id "q-221924" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $2∣(60x-45y)$ is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(0)-45(1)=-45,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Statement $9∣(60x-45y)$ is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(1)-45(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement $5∣(60x-45y)$ is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n60x-45y=5(12x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $12x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(60x-45y).$\n\nTherefore, the 