ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-216495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $n$ is odd or $n+1$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$” is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$” is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$ is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$”; $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050878 :topic/id #uuid "6de9a5a5-ab7e-5d26-821c-9f50ad7ae54d" :topic/math-academy-id 2787 :topic/title "Universal and Existential Quantifiers"} :activity/title "Universal and Existential Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407780} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407820}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge