9 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6d41efc-3f6b-5285-a299-04b9702a043c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453969} :answer-field/id #uuid "7387b7e6-f6df-542e-b959-1cad4caa776a" :answer-field/key "$33≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42227076-6750-5c0c-9261-152604f91699" :question/math-academy-id "q-76953" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $17≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$ is false because $9∤(17-2).$ Notice that $17-2=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement $23≡-10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$ is false because $5∤(23-(-10)).$ Notice that $23-(-10)=33,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement $33≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ is true because $7\\,|\\,(33-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(33-12)÷7=21÷7=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $33≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049004 :topic/id #uuid "d28e701c-f952-587a-ba2a-6c1d3807d35a" :topic/math-academy-id 2641 :topic/title "Modular Congruence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3016bf9