2}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e2b9768-6303-5aa7-8a02-db48065ae94a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98476" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1$ is true, while statement $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=3$ is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5719 & = & 2669\\cdot 2 & + & 381 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2669 & = & 381\\cdot 7 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 381 & = & 2\\cdot 190 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2 & = & 1\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $1.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $5719$ and $2669$ is $1:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$5719$ and $2669$ are coprime” is true. Since $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1,$ the numbers are coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1$; “$5719$ and $2669$ are coprime” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932 :topic/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e148fac7-727b-55d9-ba67-0a9fc7a276e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453368} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6ec723-5cd2-5173-b8f1-1778f583295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c6ec723-5cd2-5173-b8f1-1778f583295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453369} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8e1392-18d1-56b3-b0f5-ff3dd861687a" :answer-field/key "Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(\\sqrt{3},1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a536093-cb71-5d44-a52f-8e05b4b9f74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453370} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2d5ab2-a228-5241-aef5-decbffea282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc2d5ab2-a228-5241-aef5-decbffea282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453371} :answer-field/id #uuid "deacc4af-75f1-5a94-a0d0-5d1ad4ac3ea8" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})$ is defined" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b4f556-0cc0-5fc7-9fac-56ff9260d6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453372} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd91732-9818-5742-9c5d-ea668d6dbe0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd91732-9818-5742-9c5d-ea668d6dbe0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7af479-38a3-58de-bca6-c137a88e2790" :answer-field/key "${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a3b6388-98bd-5083-8d1c-f60a05890b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-132726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(\\sqrt{3},1)$” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3},1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}_{y}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})$ is defined” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined” is also true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{1})=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,1)=-\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(\\sqrt{3},1)$”; “${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})$ is defined”; “${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined”." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932 :topic/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions"} :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409538} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409564}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932 :topic/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "899f43e4-fc9a-5506-b84d-a25c2d9eb6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453928} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4690b4-12f7-5ffd-a0f0-d137fa95703c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "899f43e4-fc9a-5506-b84d-a25c2d9eb6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453928} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ccf0ae7-dc64-51f0-892e-8d88c6910e82" :answer-field/key "Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376cf739-f8d8-5cb0-91a0-52b41ea250c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453930} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcc07c3-7704-591c-aebf-ff0249e9dd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffcc07c3-7704-591c-aebf-ff0249e9dd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453931} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f29120-e8c2-52fc-abd7-8386d5f878c4" :answer-field/key "${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc77c615-3280-58d4-a19d-18282a3f7486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453932} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfc9ce4-4706-5c6b-8f71-059b43d404d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc77c615-3280-58d4-a19d-18282a3f7486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453932} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c89136-ff34-5898-9f58-5a98f17bcfb1" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72010" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$” is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined”  only'." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932 :topic/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions"} :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409538} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409560}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247 :topic/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d0f8fb4-d1b2-5bff-ba27-c6e4e679e31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453634} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7d44c8-3ad6-50d2-9658-d18a60e921c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7d44c8-3ad6-50d2-9658-d18a60e921c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453635} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba7a927-c65d-53d2-a68e-2a711452828e" :answer-field/key "$A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8c84692-3840-51b5-880d-3386d31c4f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453636} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc1d84f-0a08-51e1-9122-944928bd54c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dc1d84f-0a08-51e1-9122-944928bd54c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453637} :answer-field/id #uuid "91aa8150-4bc8-561c-9c32-8cfddb99b464" :answer-field/key "$¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f8ce0ad-e15d-5071-aae6-6ebf6f10905b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453638} {:answer/id #uuid "34bd4c0a-6891-5927-8f43-1900cd830078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f8ce0ad-e15d-5071-aae6-6ebf6f10905b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453638} :answer-field/id #uuid "728d40e4-2d8b-5727-be86-396f6263ab1f" :answer-field/key "$¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-182506" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$ is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$ is true, while statement $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$ is false. From part $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$; $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247 :topic/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements"} :activity/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409565} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409587}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/id #uuid "7fdf89be-7454-5a23-9d34-17c62e869eea" :topic/math-academy-id 2132 :topic/title "The Curl of a Vector Field"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8286c6dd-7fea-5018-b722-69a0848b0bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437502} {:answer/id #uuid "5d52abdf-7ac5-55e3-92ae-9aec71a85bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8286c6dd-7fea-5018-b722-69a0848b0bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437502} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb53bf33-5552-5395-8fd4-4d42b50a2a01" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4939bd9-e529-5af3-b533-59e09598d0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437504} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9d89e5-98a9-5520-8de9-5ddb178c0664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b9d89e5-98a9-5520-8de9-5ddb178c0664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437505} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a596bab-34c8-5ee7-9e82-c47a2988e863" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273314f4-9ed1-52ac-8d9b-6701a93d8748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437506} {:answer/id #uuid "265725b9-3d43-5f84-a380-552591b33792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "265725b9-3d43-5f84-a380-552591b33792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437507} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fd84cc-0dd3-5f03-9595-e86a0cb51c38" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc09d16-5459-5df8-9d7e-c5304315ffbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437508} {:answer/id #uuid "3423417a-d67c-5bac-8ed6-720f61a5aa60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc09d16-5459-5df8-9d7e-c5304315ffbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437508} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b1fd93-e1ee-539e-8481-cca67e61611b" :answer-field/key "$F$ is irrotational" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "656969c6-847a-533c-a9b8-d526f82e1ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105694" :question/problem "![](images/17/17637e938a25e91b9a03921dbbe0f22c8ea760f09cfed9b4a76d07bd5e3b9301.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/f3/f385add60afac473a5734f8c1ed57c6575bada03c9c16b24fed73b200c38e2c5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ is true, whereas statement $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement “$F$ is irrotational” is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$; “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/id #uuid "7fdf89be-7454-5a23-9d34-17c62e869eea" :topic/math-academy-id 2132 :topic/title "The Curl of a Vector Field"} :activity/title "The Curl of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409590} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409611}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0638bc04-e9e1-5330-9a7a-284da1ea1862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472490} {:answer/id #uuid "dedfc764-7039-5fce-9f4f-b712c657dce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dedfc764-7039-5fce-9f4f-b712c657dce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472491} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec83fef0-1275-5012-b097-d5d2217af4da" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 5\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afe1e9fe-eb97-51b8-8ce7-fcc5af4f2758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472492} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4dca9e-0f53-5ed4-ba91-c48d26d2c775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afe1e9fe-eb97-51b8-8ce7-fcc5af4f2758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472492} :answer-field/id #uuid "c35a2a7f-db4f-528f-8a21-817540e2e1e3" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<7}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473641} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9276fd3e-3c91-5a26-b6b6-d901d7767211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472494} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ed62c3-48d9-5265-ad31-aeca74a5c701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ed62c3-48d9-5265-ad31-aeca74a5c701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472495} :answer-field/id #uuid "6753bfa6-194f-5dbf-bbba-fcb3dac7d172" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x<4,\\,{y}^{2}<9}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c5afa8a-a09e-5d94-9d6f-17bde8db6cd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105422" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,\\sqrt{5}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 5\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x<4,\\,{y}^{2}<9}$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"} :activity/title "Open and Closed Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409697} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409715}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a21b5b4-8c29-55bf-9071-2d2b633aa59b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472760} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e36382-39e0-5e92-9083-d5fb9b041b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a21b5b4-8c29-55bf-9071-2d2b633aa59b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472760} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5ccf86d-3b0c-575d-be62-dcd07f2c84b2" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-2\\le x\\le 0,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 2-x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e14bf656-6f18-54c0-9140-a4e367e88751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472762} {:answer/id #uuid "ba64d4e9-e44f-50cd-ae83-6f4393f74694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba64d4e9-e44f-50cd-ae83-6f4393f74694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472763} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2d70a2-ca62-51f1-bf2b-2dd24fb3a9fd" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\\\\\,0<y<1+x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3ffa913-0456-5435-8425-10083a166104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472764} {:answer/id #uuid "e26a2e2e-02cd-579c-bae8-f4e53d04fb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ffa913-0456-5435-8425-10083a166104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472764} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f7902ed-77a2-5fae-a322-2ec4a2fe2e71" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }4\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b04f2bd5-de84-5515-a0a5-3d6c81e3d583" :question/math-academy-id "q-161644" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\\\\\,0<y<1+x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"} :activity/title "Open and Closed Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409697} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409713}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808 :topic/id #uuid "c1647abf-57b4-5851-a828-2952c6947148" :topic/math-academy-id 948 :topic/title "Indicator Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73398205-b447-5295-a67c-ad006a848004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453574} {:answer/id #uuid "66ac170a-d95d-5009-aef4-e8e0a5826587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ac170a-d95d-5009-aef4-e8e0a5826587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453575} :answer-field/id #uuid "cabc922e-c483-5964-999a-afcbcd1b58ba" :answer-field/key "${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454537} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ebd81b-61aa-58e2-8fe6-1acf2a46536f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453576} {:answer/id #uuid "c16f5c20-ce58-58cc-a4b2-847c9c364e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ebd81b-61aa-58e2-8fe6-1acf2a46536f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453576} :answer-field/id #uuid "0188fa47-67f0-516d-a06e-0f6db7d50605" :answer-field/key "${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454538} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31b9dc1f-cc8d-5093-ba46-67a90d725c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453578} {:answer/id #uuid "986b4a97-0064-5e3c-bb93-d96d0717fad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986b4a97-0064-5e3c-bb93-d96d0717fad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453579} :answer-field/id #uuid "1683ca8d-d357-5b5b-a758-4c8420293a37" :answer-field/key "$q\\in \\overset{―}{A}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09ca95e9-e7fd-5d14-80c3-ab64bc50a23d" :question/math-academy-id "q-174385" :question/problem "Given that ${1}_{A}(q)=0,$ which of the following statements about $\\overset{―}{A}$ are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint: ${1}_{A}$ denotes the indicator function of the set $A$ and ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}$ denotes the indicator function of the complement of $A.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1$ is true while statement ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=0$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q) & =1-{1}_{A}(q) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $q\\in \\overset{―}{A}$ is true. Since ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1,$ we conclude that $q$ belongs to $\\overset{―}{A}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1$; $q\\in \\overset{―}{A}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808 :topic/id #uuid "c1647abf-57b4-5851-a828-2952c6947148" :topic/math-academy-id 948 :topic/title "Indicator Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9166a2cd-e482-5ef6-8dd0-7069988d19be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453592} {:answer/id #uuid "b1393175-0d07-53d3-844c-d6ae5f6863e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1393175-0d07-53d3-844c-d6ae5f6863e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453593} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2fa57c-2909-5935-8d3c-0c6741e819f1" :answer-field/key "${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d018f001-d7c1-5d5f-ad00-a185cb6d375f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453594} {:answer/id #uuid "46f21955-ef1a-5e2c-8cda-63a6ce3b6b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d018f001-d7c1-5d5f-ad00-a185cb6d375f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453594} :answer-field/id #uuid "459b35ad-69a1-5c21-8aec-11bb813a98f2" :answer-field/key "${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454547} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f942421-ed53-502c-8912-7b5ca533e6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453596} {:answer/id #uuid "eb24302b-1253-5c54-afa0-b4ff91533d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb24302b-1253-5c54-afa0-b4ff91533d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453597} :answer-field/id #uuid "4231e57f-0807-5822-9404-6d3a419c5c18" :answer-field/key "$t\\in A\\cup B$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c60efa7-9f70-50c9-8725-0334784ed791" :question/math-academy-id "q-174402" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(t)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(t)=1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=1$ is true while statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=0$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(t),{1}_{B}(t)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $t\\in A\\cup B$ is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=1,$ we conclude that $t$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=1$; $t\\in A\\cup B$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808 :topic/id #uuid "c1647abf-57b4-5851-a828-2952c6947148" :topic/math-academy-id 948 :topic/title "Indicator Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b4cfbc8-2b8c-5140-a8bb-7f6216d1c21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453586} {:answer/id #uuid "07d45975-75ad-5ac0-80a2-a67332372a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07d45975-75ad-5ac0-80a2-a67332372a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453587} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5ce266-0ba2-521f-8f38-84d3b53bb633" :answer-field/key "${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454543} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7035d2-94ee-5d7e-baff-b2c5f0b47416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453588} {:answer/id #uuid "895992ac-82c6-5750-b799-5234f11d0dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7035d2-94ee-5d7e-baff-b2c5f0b47416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453588} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b53eb93-6623-55ce-83f0-193479b58d99" :answer-field/key "${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a494fefa-d26c-53e8-af8a-a80ac83e1e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453590} {:answer/id #uuid "d42dfa9a-5b0a-598e-8bbc-e5091eaeb0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d42dfa9a-5b0a-598e-8bbc-e5091eaeb0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453591} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c89c5b6-f3d8-532d-9821-eeff63e9eb5a" :answer-field/key "$-1\\in A\\cap B$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92b8a61e-f7a9-5e1e-adba-90e2fa6c6243" :question/math-academy-id "q-174394" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(-1)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(-1)=1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=1$ is true while statement ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=0$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{1,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $-1\\in A\\cap B$ is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=1$; $-1\\in A\\cap B$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808 :topic/id #uuid "c1647abf-57b4-5851-a828-2952c6947148" :topic/math-academy-id 948 :topic/title "Indicator Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271bd1b2-64d9-5cc7-bbec-b86ba10fc804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453568} {:answer/id #uuid "dae1f367-953f-5742-b452-749306fe9d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae1f367-953f-5742-b452-749306fe9d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453569} :answer-field/id #uuid "95b271cf-9516-5e1c-8b28-1c9dbd8c9f68" :answer-field/key "${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454534} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d4e5d4-6830-560c-878d-348a97dfcf1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453570} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8118bf-d1fb-552a-a385-b0e89891b90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05d4e5d4-6830-560c-878d-348a97dfcf1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453570} :answer-field/id #uuid "0189df02-b3e6-5072-80bc-52efb3c878fc" :answer-field/key "${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454535} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9329cfe9-e21e-5113-b79d-99313f7bb970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453572} {:answer/id #uuid "d680e758-2e50-5500-9ac7-2a9c9e252fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d680e758-2e50-5500-9ac7-2a9c9e252fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453573} :answer-field/id #uuid "6302a2ae-7699-51e6-afdc-d665c6513e54" :answer-field/key "$0\\in \\overset{―}{A}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8a05322-998a-5d01-8b89-68a8bc93dd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-174380" :question/problem "Given that ${1}_{A}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements about $\\overset{―}{A}$ are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint: ${1}_{A}$ denotes the indicator function of the set $A$ and ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}$ denotes the indicator function of the complement of $A.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1$ is true while statement ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=0$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0) & =1-{1}_{A}(0) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $0\\in \\overset{―}{A}$ is true. Since ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1,$ we conclude that $0$ belongs to $\\overset{―}{A}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1$; $0\\in \\overset{―}{A}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccbbfd1c-6a08-5dc0-ae78-4e44e801c525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454084} {:answer/id #uuid "4a04030c-b721-5bf3-be03-2ecb5763411f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccbbfd1c-6a08-5dc0-ae78-4e44e801c525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef9254a-d85b-51ec-9bb3-1270c8e3b239" :answer-field/key "The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,-2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454792} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a872d88-d4b7-519b-807e-1141297b7849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454086} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd8f61b-4e41-5b3f-9d99-08b8822233ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdd8f61b-4e41-5b3f-9d99-08b8822233ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454087} :answer-field/id #uuid "accd4fd1-1699-5f0d-895e-42caceb1cf69" :answer-field/key "$∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,2],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300e70b0-e346-5a85-b265-dcd48829a1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454088} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e966b5-2ff2-5233-acc6-ce4cf8c0ff68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e966b5-2ff2-5233-acc6-ce4cf8c0ff68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454089} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2a2f00-559b-589b-b61f-d44d5d05df46" :answer-field/key "$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a60aead-59e2-5a19-8c88-b84da12bb8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-90823" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/3575afe4fd4e5aa6754496154986b272a43fb5c035ec8d69621d52fad16ee1ea.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,-2]$” is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2,$ and it occurs at the point $(0,2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values lower than $y=2$, including $y=2$ itself. So the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,2],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2]$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$” is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=3\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,2],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$”; “$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8ea4da6-07e1-57b7-a83b-7aaa01037d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472472} {:answer/id #uuid "4b72b35d-e95a-594a-9f80-c223cf007810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ea4da6-07e1-57b7-a83b-7aaa01037d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472472} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b0b7b5-216a-546d-8ada-6354495eb0a1" :answer-field/key "$f(1)=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473631} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62bb0d7-c664-52e5-962a-8f1a4a27c301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472474} {:answer/id #uuid "987df7de-06e3-5899-9ac7-26468bcfaf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987df7de-06e3-5899-9ac7-26468bcfaf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472475} :answer-field/id #uuid "26d4a480-241b-594b-a24f-7feaa874aaa8" :answer-field/key "$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f73c605-275a-55b6-8e5a-e9bd0344391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472476} {:answer/id #uuid "95048fe1-1692-52bf-b42e-39fccbdf29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95048fe1-1692-52bf-b42e-39fccbdf29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472477} :answer-field/id #uuid "046b1c2d-c127-5bfe-9da5-5e118283b7ee" :answer-field/key "The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1346b272-ab34-50a3-8e2c-0cbfe3ad7ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105231" :question/problem "![](images/37/373448202bfd022f5629219b2c176f4096e4eeae275c1095798d77be92122cc2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(1)=4$ is false. There is an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $2\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)=2\\ne 4.$\n\n- Statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is true. According to the diagram, $4\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement “The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**” is true. Since statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$”; “The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c7a1d5-2624-5583-aeae-da6b722a9800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473548} {:answer/id #uuid "d811fc76-0af3-5686-b258-9ec94ee426b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42c7a1d5-2624-5583-aeae-da6b722a9800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473548} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bfbf6e-2545-5f52-9799-41c7bca12360" :answer-field/key "$f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81660a94-f669-50bb-b01a-01293ad51c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473550} {:answer/id #uuid "51f4519c-df1c-5ed7-887b-6f1eca40896e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81660a94-f669-50bb-b01a-01293ad51c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473550} :answer-field/id #uuid "30d06d02-6bed-5ad7-8f32-70dcdb1c3125" :answer-field/key "$g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f56e9ea-8cf4-54d7-8527-7f8faf227ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473552} {:answer/id #uuid "4a669770-c352-5a86-88f4-78cbb1e23d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a669770-c352-5a86-88f4-78cbb1e23d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473553} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9581ab-c4fe-5bba-9e72-95a487a5dfad" :answer-field/key "$h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2021b7c7-5d84-5929-874c-8d6923b11db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-90878" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- “$f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$” is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f(\\sqrt[3]{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{3}=y.$\n\n- “$g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$” is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={e}^{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g({e}^{y})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y})=y.$\n\n- “$h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$” is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015d78ce-10e6-5aa2-8e40-5fcea0882254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453168} {:answer/id #uuid "00556d4d-0185-5264-9f2a-aa2a6f260ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "015d78ce-10e6-5aa2-8e40-5fcea0882254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453168} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74da5de-c05b-5fd4-ab1e-e42243e3aba7" :answer-field/key "The range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0f6634-264d-54c2-8e61-ed2f2e5776a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453170} {:answer/id #uuid "daa3ffd2-014c-5bfb-8d81-f8a7e9466f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e0f6634-264d-54c2-8e61-ed2f2e5776a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453170} :answer-field/id #uuid "83459419-3597-52d7-9759-0b3f9e59c3aa" :answer-field/key "$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454335} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef48bf4-b61d-5d18-8d06-67153f8f0864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453172} {:answer/id #uuid "aead6e6b-ab86-52e2-ab5e-8e74bd9b4b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aead6e6b-ab86-52e2-ab5e-8e74bd9b4b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453173} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c719c4e-9fc3-558a-a32d-6604969ef25f" :answer-field/key "$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39b57e8a-5e0f-5758-8d6b-0d9667e7e35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105258" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={2}^{-x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/c7/c79c1123036c978c8c4da514651c0bb1c8e3df34c8bbaff099bee601d568ed10.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty )$” is false. The graph will cover all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$” is true. Since statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fddfaf89-9322-5a84-a50e-62aa07b28c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473560} {:answer/id #uuid "551a09d1-398a-58c1-94f9-f0fdcf86b67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "551a09d1-398a-58c1-94f9-f0fdcf86b67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473561} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10319f6-e3ce-51db-9398-cfeb826e35f6" :answer-field/key "$f:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e06e772-1e77-53fb-909f-e91be4a700e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473562} {:answer/id #uuid "7d750378-c7c6-5ba3-beb9-58671cf4ce69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e06e772-1e77-53fb-909f-e91be4a700e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473562} :answer-field/id #uuid "639df046-b26b-596d-ab4d-ff5cf881eb13" :answer-field/key "$g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "483d3aae-3043-5e83-859a-284e4fd1571c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473564} {:answer/id #uuid "515754aa-883f-54ef-9b07-757603046125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "515754aa-883f-54ef-9b07-757603046125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dfe423e-2133-5abd-80c5-484043bc3e63" :answer-field/key "$h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fb6cc00-0fe3-5564-afaa-6bddf30c9f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-90955" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- “$f:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$” is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- “$g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$” is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g(\\sqrt{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{2}=y.$\n\n- “$h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$” is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$”; “$h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f4b9fce-88a2-5c65-8752-31bb8c08b10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454090} {:answer/id #uuid "1c77a378-a0f8-5768-8e57-d661a10c42dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b9fce-88a2-5c65-8752-31bb8c08b10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454090} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae3c14ef-feb4-50b1-b173-09d5116caffe" :answer-field/key "The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fd58017-e4ba-5b47-983d-1e652dcc9e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454092} {:answer/id #uuid "d49b29d6-d5d4-51be-84b7-33db7b3b848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d49b29d6-d5d4-51be-84b7-33db7b3b848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454093} :answer-field/id #uuid "35de4aba-fc6c-5fa8-a181-9eb17458aad4" :answer-field/key "$∀\\,y\\in R$ $∃\\,x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00096cbf-a739-5274-b425-3e305c30dad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454094} {:answer/id #uuid "80dd2edb-196d-5524-841f-8e00d6c94a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00096cbf-a739-5274-b425-3e305c30dad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454094} :answer-field/id #uuid "df7bb5e2-b8b1-56ec-86e5-324f98f8a216" :answer-field/key "$f$ maps $(0,\\infty )$ **into** $R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4110bb56-279f-50bf-96a1-301ebf1eb3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90859" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3e074a2d029aeb3f4016332b9fb762fff6ec60ed7dce0290f97a95d6cb57c1.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$” is false. From the graph, there is no highest or lowest $y$ value. The graph will cover any real value $y.$ So the range of the function is all $R..$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,y\\in R$ $∃\\,x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=y$” is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ maps $(0,\\infty )$ **into** $R$” is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$ the function is a surjection. Therefore, $f$ is *not* an into function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∀\\,y\\in R$ $∃\\,x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=y$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c93ca70a-47da-5baf-851c-e1bf791c7b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453174} {:answer/id #uuid "64807ced-aa57-58a5-ae0d-ac029c59ac34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64807ced-aa57-58a5-ae0d-ac029c59ac34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453175} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4fceb5-932c-5132-bb87-d2badbb9ddb9" :answer-field/key "The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c61cf70-41be-5318-bf2e-74949ba6f18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453176} {:answer/id #uuid "236cd33b-d523-510a-84cc-440d53a1b398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c61cf70-41be-5318-bf2e-74949ba6f18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453176} :answer-field/id #uuid "39af1c63-d29d-5ade-b582-b9d3d244610e" :answer-field/key "$∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ef1019c-6076-59b2-b8b1-dfd04b1ded3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453178} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6c31df-bd28-538d-b442-6e9f77cc33df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ef1019c-6076-59b2-b8b1-dfd04b1ded3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453178} :answer-field/id #uuid "5543a8bb-3812-58e8-8791-6e292fe8e1f3" :answer-field/key "$f$ is a function of $R$ **into** $(-\\infty ,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b62af08-9109-5599-b006-f2ceb899c9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-105263" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to (-\\infty ,1)$ which is given by $f(x)=1-{2}^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35bd21eef06d1ea63aba6e420d9842de61f9248c4fa26c2895f6825f4e7a3848.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$” is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values lower than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1)$,  there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $1\\notin (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement “$f$ is a function of $R$ **into** $(-\\infty ,1)$” is false. Since statement “The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$” is true, the function is a surjection.  So, $f$ is *not* a function of $R$ *into* $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe1a6204-e5ec-5f44-b26e-7ccc55915e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473542} {:answer/id #uuid "ae086f9f-b92c-5aab-8c76-5c009e5e2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae086f9f-b92c-5aab-8c76-5c009e5e2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9131fc67-75e4-563c-b8d0-94a23a385951" :answer-field/key "$f(2)=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "791d06be-d390-539a-ac0c-06e35b4d0cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473544} {:answer/id #uuid "fad30099-8449-5c4a-8a50-0b3446240d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "791d06be-d390-539a-ac0c-06e35b4d0cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473544} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37b982e-6668-5b7d-b9a0-36b334e068a8" :answer-field/key "$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa15fe7e-8ae5-55d8-95d5-857c052ca9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473546} {:answer/id #uuid "0098fe9d-5f1e-546e-a0ec-56802952db84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa15fe7e-8ae5-55d8-95d5-857c052ca9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d089faf-62fc-5a4e-ad21-0bf3a5219729" :answer-field/key "The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae359058-a9c8-58ca-9940-1c14f3b8e228" :question/math-academy-id "q-90789" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f68e70397b982a70b372b2bd9e4f73da3374d3abfb2ef4bbd2ec1fffe6dbb31.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(2)=4$ is true. Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement “The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**” is false. Since statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(2)=4$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc41b65-ba1b-56a8-b545-ecd45b401396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473554} {:answer/id #uuid "da2eb5f7-1baa-53f4-ad79-0d1d55247851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc41b65-ba1b-56a8-b545-ecd45b401396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473554} :answer-field/id #uuid "74710deb-3884-595d-a92d-aa098b3250f9" :answer-field/key "$f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b0aebe-c007-541d-b409-83571ec2c6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473556} {:answer/id #uuid "07e54daa-2cf8-5d76-b9fc-ac27c1ee1152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b0aebe-c007-541d-b409-83571ec2c6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473556} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55e82c7-9120-59da-8986-81de94c17491" :answer-field/key "$h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d8197f8-ea17-52e0-ada2-12a7ef638766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473558} {:answer/id #uuid "579573c7-4c0d-580e-a0b2-b9f7c31c308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "579573c7-4c0d-580e-a0b2-b9f7c31c308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473559} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f071064-8b41-5c17-83e0-821190cb7676" :answer-field/key "$g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d76a2452-4488-5b99-ace5-042b8edc218d" :question/math-academy-id "q-90902" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- “$f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$” is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={2}^{-y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $f(x)=f({2}^{-y})=-{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{-y})=y.$\n\n- “$h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$” is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$” is an into function. Let's take $y=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}=y.$ Indeed, in this case we would have \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x}=y\\,⇔\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\,⇔\\,x=\\sqrt{2}\\in Q,\n$$\n\n which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532ae303-67bd-57f2-938f-bbed36329b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472478} {:answer/id #uuid "fb500e02-911c-5c8b-9f78-e88f6e8c72fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "532ae303-67bd-57f2-938f-bbed36329b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472478} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cec2b1e-72b6-50a1-855d-2902cfa078a9" :answer-field/key "$f(1)=7$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7073623d-5ce7-509c-a188-db7013873ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472480} {:answer/id #uuid "09b1b2c0-e961-5267-b086-8cb73af100c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b1b2c0-e961-5267-b086-8cb73af100c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472481} :answer-field/id #uuid "d770e4eb-8dda-5703-95a9-2c2d291bcb35" :answer-field/key "$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473635} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bb4560-454b-5e25-81d8-5d5901fcb0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472482} {:answer/id #uuid "42ef5f9d-dc43-5572-9241-468f3a9c6c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ef5f9d-dc43-5572-9241-468f3a9c6c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472483} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b741a97-1773-51a6-a938-029c480ebbf0" :answer-field/key "The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e45af0c1-a25f-5254-88c0-adcdabddd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-105246" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae980a05fc0d25d2696185c88fcf90ff156af9c3f42af7cece80f8c33692a3d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(1)=7$ is false. There is no arrow from $1\\in X$ to $7\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)\\ne 7.$\n\n- Statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is true. According to the diagram, $5\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $5\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=5$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement “The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**” is true. Since statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$”; “The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866 :topic/title "The Dimension of a Span"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae48163-0998-5b3d-b29e-8d844bd75e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453454} {:answer/id #uuid "fac7d0b5-081a-56e0-901b-a2ae495d04aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ae48163-0998-5b3d-b29e-8d844bd75e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d31b9f46-6bca-5c00-88d4-25fd24f38387" :answer-field/key "${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8a92446-e8c8-5739-82ef-d0ba00e62ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453456} {:answer/id #uuid "00c70672-536d-5c0e-84ab-327beb9e056e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00c70672-536d-5c0e-84ab-327beb9e056e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453457} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6e49be-3808-5326-8880-c9624d3a62b4" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454478} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d46f9ac4-f8c7-5cc1-9675-07387c704c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453458} {:answer/id #uuid "325a6d83-0f48-5758-9374-3733e1e6f1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d46f9ac4-f8c7-5cc1-9675-07387c704c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453458} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea1fc63-07a4-50e6-a7e4-57b3bf645806" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159782" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$” is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$ is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$ is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402ff24c-ce68-5100-bc6f-ce08b50c1b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437378} {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0832f4-ea5d-510d-80b2-f2b601f5cea1" :answer-field/key "$F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} {:answer/id #uuid "aadf4a2f-a9fb-5a4e-a7c3-c50425746fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} :answer-field/id #uuid "245451c8-c2d8-5660-b868-914d6a09643c" :answer-field/key "$G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3af17a-9e86-50af-944e-c33c42e3d28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437382} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd21fc02-2d66-5119-b27a-c74692938a6c" :answer-field/key "$H$ $=(8x-3y)\\,i$ $+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104574" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$; $H$ $=(8x-3y)\\,i$ $+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"} :activity/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410562} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410577}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519cca11-1af0-5c38-ac09-9cdf3e9eb8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439999} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3485904-6c14-5182-a07e-0e8536e160b9" :answer-field/key "$F$ $=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i$ $+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb20b90e-716b-5744-aa1c-1bc38cc09c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440001} {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002} :answer-field/id #uuid "908db421-59c3-5473-9555-1804770c666a" :answer-field/key "$G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e2896f-89e3-56ed-85e3-9569e0e553b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440003} {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bd3f31-bc54-5cfa-8930-506cacf26495" :answer-field/key "$H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86896" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F$ $=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i$ $+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$; $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$; $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"} :activity/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410562} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410579}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723 :topic/id #uuid "0e15397e-6c32-508c-b6ea-90f1a9e5a237" :topic/math-academy-id 1904 :topic/title "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3fbcf3-b021-5e3b-a8b7-2522323e0024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574251} {:answer/id #uuid "f66f0582-d02f-5062-ba40-45eee5610cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f66f0582-d02f-5062-ba40-45eee5610cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574252} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2e64d8-97e6-58dd-97a2-16b6a551fd1d" :answer-field/key "Column 1: $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96f34365-9234-50f0-8104-124cf2b5ed96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574253} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd5f958-fb28-56c4-ab99-9c5f4aeb0df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96f34365-9234-50f0-8104-124cf2b5ed96" :answer/type {:db/id #